     On transmit rings, after a nneettmmaapp system call, slots in the range
     _h_e_a_d ... _t_a_i_l_-_1 are available for transmission.  User code should fill
     the slots sequentially and advance _h_e_a_d and _c_u_r past slots ready to
     transmit.  _c_u_r may be moved further ahead if the user code needs more
     slots before further transmissions (see _S_C_A_T_T_E_R _G_A_T_H_E_R _I_/_O).

     At the next NIOCTXSYNC/select()/poll(), slots up to _h_e_a_d_-_1 are pushed to
     the port, and _t_a_i_l may advance if further slots have become available.
     Below is an example of the evolution of a TX ring:

         after the syscall, slots between cur and tail are (a)vailable
                   head=cur   tail
                    |          |
                    v          v
          TX  [.....aaaaaaaaaaa.............]

         user creates new packets to (T)ransmit
                     head=cur tail
                         |     |
                         v     v
          TX  [.....TTTTTaaaaaa.............]

         NIOCTXSYNC/poll()/select() sends packets and reports new slots
                     head=cur      tail
                         |          |
                         v          v
          TX  [..........aaaaaaaaaaa........]

     sseelleecctt() and ppoollll() will block if there is no space in the ring, i.e.,
           ring->cur == ring->tail
     and return when new slots have become available.

     High speed applications may want to amortize the cost of system calls by
     preparing as many packets as possible before issuing them.

     A transmit ring with pending transmissions has
           ring->head != ring->tail + 1 (modulo the ring size).
     The function _i_n_t _n_m___t_x___p_e_n_d_i_n_g_(_r_i_n_g_) implements this test.

